.TH MUX 1 "" "mux 0.32" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mux \- a tmux session manager for agent-heavy, many-session work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mux
.RI [ option "] [" verb "] [" NAME "] [" PROFILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mux
builds and drives tmux sessions from declarative
.B layouts
and puts a live status bar on top: each session's coding-agent state at a
glance (idle, working, blocked), a per-host colour chip, and \(em through an
optional
.B context
hook \(em a marker for whichever side of a work / personal boundary a pane
sits on.
.PP
A session is created from a layout of the same name (or an explicit one), its
panes opened at a shared root, one running an
.BR agent .
Sessions are picked, cycled, and jumped-to by how much attention each agent
needs, so "who is waiting on me" is always visible.
.PP
mux ships a tmux fragment (see
.BR "TMUX INTEGRATION" )
that wires the status bar, key bindings, and hooks; the
.B mux
command manages sessions, layouts, themes, and the palette.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mux
With no verb, pick a session to attach (an fzf picker, else a numbered menu).
.TP
.B mux go " [" \fINAME\fR "] [" \fILAYOUT\fR ]
Create, attach, or switch to session
.I NAME
(default: derived from the current directory), building it from
.I PROFILE
if it is not up. The agent
.B continues
its most recent conversation.
.TP
.B mux go \-\-resume " [" \fINAME\fR "] [" \fILAYOUT\fR ]
As
.BR go ,
but the agent
.B resumes
\(em it prompts to choose a conversation. Only meaningful when the session is
being built. This was once the bare verb
.BR "mux resume" ;
that name now belongs to the session rebuild below, and the flag is the only
spelling.
.TP
.B mux \-\-no\-agent " ..."
Build the session but run neither the agent's
.I go
nor
.I resume
command \(em a plain shell in the agent pane.
.RB ( \-\-bare
is kept as an alias.)
.TP
.B mux ls
List sessions in the current context, each tagged with its agent-state glyph.
.TP
.B mux new " " \fINAME\fR
Create session
.I NAME
rooted at the CURRENT directory, and bind the name so
.B mux go \fINAME\fR
finds it again. The one EXPLICIT creation verb:
.B go
creates implicitly whenever it can point at evidence the target exists (a
profile, a scanned repository, or the directory you are in), and refuses a typed
name that nothing knows, because from the input alone a new session and a typo
are identical. A row is written only when
.I NAME
is not what this directory already derives.
.TP
.B mux resume " [" \-\-list ]
Rebuild every session this partition had recorded, then attach the first.
Sessions are recorded automatically as you open them and forgotten on
.BR "mux kill" ,
so after a reboot this brings back what you had. It acts on the CURRENT
partition and takes no way to name another. The set is state, not config:
.IR $MUX_CACHE/sessions.<socket> ,
one
.RI NAME <TAB> ROOT
per line, per machine and never in git.
.TP
.B mux go \-\-attach\-only " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Attach
.I NAME
if it is live, and REFUSE if it is not, rather than building it. Exits 1 naming
the session. Written for
.BR "mux latch" ,
which uses it on every attempt after the first so that a rebooted host is
reported rather than silently replaced with an empty session.
.TP
.B mux latch " " \fIHOST\fR [: \fISESSION\fR ]
Hold an attachment to a mux session on another machine open across network
drops, bringing your own transport. It reports what it is waiting on rather
than retrying in silence, and refuses to retry the two cases where retrying is
wrong: a session the human deliberately quit, and a credential the far side
rejected. See
.B LATCH
for the state machine and the seams.
.TP
.B mux scan " [" \-\-list ]
Rebuild the project discovery map for this context, or print it. The map is a
cache: it also rebuilds on first use and on a lookup miss, never on a timer.
.TP
.B mux save " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Snapshot the current session, recording only what mux could not have derived:
the arrangement as
.IR layouts/NAME .layout
unless it already matches an existing layout, and a row carrying
.B root
only when the name would not derive from it,
.B theme
only when it is not the one the name hashes to, and
.B layout
only when it is not
.BR default .
So saving a session you never rearranged writes nothing.
.TP
.B mux edit " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Open a profile in
.BR $EDITOR .
A row is presented as a multi-line draft and folded back into a line on save. A
draft returned with a COMMENT in it, or with a value containing a space, is
promoted to
.I $MUX_DIR/profiles.d/<name>.profile
instead and its row dropped. A draft that does not parse is kept and reported;
editing the same name resumes it.
.TP
.B mux rename " [" \fIOLD\fR "] " \fINEW\fR
Rename a session and its matching layout.
.TP
.B mux theme " [" \fINAME\fR " | next | prev]"
Show, set, or cycle the current session's colour theme. Setting one REMEMBERS
it: a theme chosen by hand is a decision, and the profile is the only place
decisions live, so it is written immediately rather than behind a flag you have
to recall. A breakout profile takes it in place; everything else becomes a table
row.
.RB ( \-p / \-\-persist
is retired \(em accepted and ignored, since it is now the default.)
.TP
.B mux next-blocked
Jump to the session whose agent has needed you longest \(em the loudest chip on
the strip. Repeat to walk down the urgency order.
.TP
.B mux hide " " \fISESSION\fR ", " mux show " " \fISESSION\fR
Hide or unhide
.I SESSION
for this client only \(em it drops off this attachment's status strip and out of
its
.RB ( "/" )
cycling. Per client, so monitors can differ.
.TP
.B mux show-all
Clear this client's hidden sessions.
.TP
.B mux reload
Re-source
.I tmux.conf
on every running mux server (recompiling the palette), so a config or theme edit
takes effect without a restart.
.TP
.B mux kill " " \fINAME\fR ", " mux kill-all
Tear down the named session, or (with a prompt) every session on this socket.
Killing is also how the session set FORGETS a session, and it is the only thing
that prunes it. A name that is merely RECORDED \(em its session already gone,
its root moved or deleted \(em is killable too: there is nothing to tear down,
but it stops
.B mux resume
reporting it as unbuildable on every run. A name that is neither live nor
recorded is an error.
.SS Diagnostics and maintenance
.TP
.B mux why " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Print every value mux resolved and WHERE each came from: the directory you are
in, the context token and its source, the partition, the session name, the
profile backing it (a table row, a breakout file, or none), and the root, theme,
layout and agent with the provenance of each.
.IP
It answers for what
.B mux go
would do with the same argument, which is the point of it. A typed NAME is
resolved through the session set and then the discovery map \(em the same two
sources go uses \(em so an AMBIGUOUS name reports as ambiguous and lists its
candidates, and a name nothing knows reports as unknown, each saying that go
would refuse rather than describing a session mux will not build.
.TP
.B mux views " [" auto | floor | ceil | next "] [" \-\-detach " " \fICLIENT\fR ]
Who is attached to this server, at what size, and what that costs.
.IP
tmux sizes a window to ONE client. With two attached at DIFFERENT dimensions
there is no size that suits both, so windows resize as you cycle sessions and
every mux layout is re-pinned each time \(em the usual cause being an ssh
window left attached at another height. mux calls that VIEW TENSION, shows it
on the status bar while it lasts, and names the ways out:
.RS
.TP
.B auto
The window follows the last client to use it. Every view is right for whoever
is looking, and the price is that it moves. tmux's default
.RB ( window\-size " " latest ).
.TP
.B floor
Every window fits the SMALLEST client. Stable, nothing is ever clipped, and a
bigger view carries dead rows
.RB ( smallest ).
.TP
.B ceil
Every window fits the LARGEST client. Stable, the big view is perfect, and a
smaller one is clipped
.RB ( largest ).
.RE
.IP
No mode is correct in general; each buys stability with something. With no
argument the current clients, their idle times and the mode are reported;
.B next
cycles, which is also what CLICKING the indicator does.
.IP
That indicator is a single glyph at the right end of the status strip, always
present so the bar never changes width and the mode stays legible when nothing
is contending. It carries both dimensions at once:
.B SHAPE
is the mode you chose: a heavy bar with a stem, where the BAR is the pin and
its position is the bound \(em below for
.BR floor ,
above for
.BR ceil
\(em and a heavy asterisk for
.BR auto ,
which is not pinned at all. The asterisk is deliberate: every up\-down arrow
actually present in a monospace face renders too small to read at a glance, and
the bold ones arrive through font fallback at another face's metrics. The HEAVY
one rather than the plain one because the plain asterisk is drawn near the cap
line, floating above the glyphs beside it. Meanwhile
.B COLOUR
is what is happening to THIS view: grey when nothing contends for this window,
white when it is the one setting the size, amber when it carries dead rows, and
the caution pairing when it is CLIPPED and part of the window is off screen. The
colour is PER CLIENT: two clients on one session see different colours, because
the question it answers is what each of them is losing.
.IP
Contention is scoped to the WINDOW, not the server. tmux sizes a window from
the clients attached to its SESSION, so two differently\-sized clients sitting
on two different sessions contend over nothing and the mode changes nothing for
either \(em the indicator stays grey and says so.
.B mux views
reports the server\-wide picture as well, and says plainly whether it bites the
window you are looking at.
.IP
Note a client's reported HEIGHT includes its status line(s); the window gets
what is left. mux accounts for that, so the client actually setting the size
reads as fit rather than one row short.
.B \-\-detach
removes a client, ending its claim on the size altogether.
.TP
.B mux agent-doctor " [" \fINS\fR ]
Does each session's RECORDED agent state match what its agent is doing? The
status strip reports state from per\-pane files written by an agent's lifecycle
hooks; when a hook does not fire, the file keeps saying whatever it last said
and nothing on the bar can contradict it, because the bar IS the file. This
asks the other side \(em whether an agent process under that pane is burning
CPU \(em and reports any disagreement.
.IP
It also asks the REVERSE question: is an agent running with no record at all?
That case is the more damaging of the two and was long invisible here. A stale
record shows the WRONG state, but it shows something; a MISSING one shows
nothing, and the strip draws nothing exactly as it draws a session you never
started an agent in. Such a session is reported as an
.BR ORPHAN .
It heals on that agent's next lifecycle hook, but a session idle for days fires
none until you interact with it.
.IP
It also settles the case CPU alone cannot. A record saying
.B working
with no CPU is ambiguous: an agent waiting on the model or on a slow build is
genuinely mid\-turn AND idle on the processor, so that has always been reported
as
.B quiet
rather than risk crying wolf. But a record left saying
.B working
after the turn ENDED looks identical from CPU alone, and that is a real bug seen
twice in one day on two machines. Such a session is reported
.BR STALE .
.IP
The tiebreaker is the agent's own interface. An
.I agents/<name>.agent
definition may declare a
.B busy
line: a string that appears in the pane only while a turn runs. Asking the AGENT
DEFINITION keeps mux neutral, since mux never learns what any particular agent's
interface says. An agent that declares no marker, or a pane that cannot be
captured, leaves the verdict exactly where it was \(em guessing would make this
confidently wrong about a working agent.
.IP
Non\-zero exit when a session reports a finished agent that is working, when a
record outlives the turn it described, or when any agent is running unrecorded.
.IP
STRICTLY READ\-ONLY. It writes nothing and removes nothing, unlike
.BR "mux agent-render" ,
which prunes state files it believes orphaned and so is destructive when it
cannot see the server. Use this to inspect state, never the renderer.
.IP
CPU alone cannot settle every case, so there are three bands, not two: clearly
idle, a middle band reported but not counted, and clearly working.
.B MUX_DOCTOR_WINDOW
sets the sampling seconds;
.B MUX_DOCTOR_BUSY_PCT
and
.B MUX_DOCTOR_SUSPECT_PCT
set the thresholds.
.TP
.B mux agent-emit " \fISTATE\fR [" \-\-beat ]
Record this pane's agent state, called from an agent's lifecycle hooks, never by
hand. STATE is
.BR idle ", " working " or " blocked .
Silent on stdout, because it runs inside a hook where output would be injected
into the model's context.
.IP
.B \-\-beat
marks the caller a HEARTBEAT rather than a turn start. A beat says "still
going", which is meaningless if the heart already stopped: it may refresh an
existing
.B working
but must never resurrect one out of
.BR idle .
.IP
That distinction is load-bearing, and its absence was a live bug.
.B idle
means THE TURN ENDED, and only a turn start may leave it. Any event that fires
AFTER a turn finishes \(em a trailing
.BR PostToolUse ,
a
.BR SubagentStop ,
a post-turn summary \(em otherwise writes
.B working
as a fresh transition, and since nothing fires again afterwards it sticks
forever. The session then shows a working glyph while sitting at an empty
prompt waiting for you, which is worse than the reverse: a session that looks
BUSY is one you deliberately leave alone, so the wait is unbounded.
.IP
So a hook that means "a turn started" passes no flag, and a hook that only
witnesses activity passes
.BR \-\-beat .
A
.B blocked
to
.B working
promotion is still allowed with
.BR \-\-beat ,
since approving a permission prompt genuinely resumes work.
.TP
.B mux agent-list " [" \fINS\fR ]
Per\-session agent state, machine readable, one line each:
.IP
.EX
<state> <age\-seconds> <SESSION>
.EE
.IP
Where
.B mux agent-summary
collapses a namespace to a single worst\-state\-and\-count line for a tray icon,
this keeps every session \(em what a tooltip, a right\-click menu, or a tray
aggregating SEVERAL MACHINES needs. A session with more than one agent pane
reports its worst state, so nothing hides behind an idle sibling.
.IP
SESSION comes last, the same rule the state record follows, so a name
containing a space reads back whole with one
.BR "read -r st age sess" .
.IP
An AGE, not an epoch, and that is deliberate. The intended caller is
.BR "ssh " \fIhost\fR " mux agent-list" ,
and a reader on another machine must never subtract a remote clock's timestamp
from its own now: a box a few seconds out would misreport every duration, and
one badly out would report negative ones. The age is resolved on the host that
wrote the record, so what crosses the wire is already an answer. A record with
no usable timestamp reports an age of
.B \-
rather than being dropped (which would hide a live agent) or given a fabricated
zero.
.IP
THE EXIT STATUS IS PART OF THE CONTRACT. A host with no agents prints nothing
and exits ZERO. This command has no failure of its own, so a non\-zero exit can
only have come from the transport \(em an unreachable box, no
.B mux
on the remote PATH \(em and a presenter MUST render that as unknown rather than
as calm. A host that is down but draws as idle is how you stop checking a
machine that needed you.
.IP
Read\-only and headless: derived from the state files, never from
.BR tmux ,
so it answers with no server, no client and no
.BR $TMUX .
.TP
.B mux agent-summary " [" \fINS\fR ]
One line,
.BR "<state> <count>" :
the worst live state across the namespace and how many sessions sit in it, or
.B "none 0"
when nothing is tracked. The feed the tray indicator polls. Same derivation and
same ranking as
.BR "mux agent-list" .
.TP
.B mux check
Audit the install: tmux, mux's own presence on PATH, the desktop notification
path, the shipped package data, the config overlay, and the scan roots for this
partition. One
.BR [OK] / [WARN] / [FAIL]
line per check and a non\-zero exit on any FAIL. The markers are a string
convention, not shared code, so an integrator can style them with its own
palette.
.IP
It also asks whether mux is WORKING, not merely installed, because everything
above is a presence check and a presence check says
.B [OK]
to a box that is fully installed and fully broken. So it asks tmux what it is
actually configured to do: that prefix
.BR ( ", " ) ", " b ", " r " and " R
are bound to mux verbs rather than to whatever tmux left there, and that
.I status\-right
and
.I status\-left
are still mux's own jobs. With no server for this partition these are skipped
\(em a healthy state on a fresh boot or a headless box \(em and the skip is
reported rather than passed over in silence.
.IP
These are READ\-ONLY queries. The check deliberately never runs
.BR "mux agent-render" ,
which prunes state files and, pointed at the wrong socket, has deleted every
agent's state. An audit must not be able to damage what it inspects.
.IP
Two
.B mux
commands on PATH is a FAIL: the earlier entry shadows the later one and then
rots behind it, and no other check here can see that, because every other check
follows the copy it is already running from. Auditing a tree OTHER than the one
PATH resolves to is a WARN, since running a development checkout's
.I ./bin/mux check
does exactly that on purpose.
.TP
.B mux help " [" \fITOPIC\fR ]
The usage summary, or a topic in depth:
.BR agents ", " themes ", " profiles ", " palette .
.TP
.B mux migrate-profiles " [" \-\-apply ]
Convert pre\-rename
.I *.layout
files to the profile table. Reports what it would do; \-\-apply performs it.
Needed once, by a config predating the profile/layout split.
.SS Addressing a session
A session is named by its
.I NAME
or by its
.IR DIR :
anything containing a slash, a leading tilde, or a bare
.B .
is a path, since a session name never contains one. A path is EVIDENCE, so it
never reaches the unknown\-name refusal, and an exact\-root claim wins over
climbing to the git toplevel \(em a subdirectory session resolves to itself,
not to its enclosing repository. This is the form
.B mux resume
replays, so resuming is a loop over a public command rather than a private
path through the resolver.
.SH PROFILES AND LAYOUTS
A
.B profile
is the session: its identity and appearance. It lives at
.IR "$MUX_DIR/<name>" .profile,
and the FILENAME is the session name. Every directive is optional, and they are
last-wins and order-independent:
.TP
.B root \fIDIR\fR
Working directory for every pane (a leading
.B ~
expands).
.TP
.B theme \fINAME\fR
The colour theme (see
.BR THEMES ).
.TP
.B agent \fINAME\fR
Which agent profile a
.B pane agent
runs (see
.BR AGENTS ).
.TP
.B notify \fIalways\fR | \fIaway\fR
Whether the agent notification fires always, or only while you are away.
.TP
.B layout \fINAME\fR
The pane arrangement to build. Default
.BR default .
.PP
A
.B layout
is the pane arrangement, and only that:
.IR NAME .layout
under
.IR layouts/ ,
in
.B $MUX_DIR
or shipped in
.BR $MUX_SHARE ,
the former winning on a name collision.
.TP
.B window \fINAME\fR
Start a new window. The first one opens the session.
.TP
.B pane \fICMD\fR
A pane running
.IR CMD .
.B pane agent
runs the session's agent,
.B pane \fINAME\fR
a specific agent, and a bare
.B pane
a plain shell.
.TP
.B bottom \fIN\fR | \fIMIN\-MAX\fR
A full-width bottom shell pane, with a fixed or bounded height in lines.
.PP
The split is enforced: an arrangement directive in a profile, or an identity
directive in a layout, fails loud rather than half-working. With no profile at
all, mux roots the session at the project you are in and builds the shipped
.B default
layout, so nothing needs configuring for
.B mux go
to work.
.SH THEMES
The palette is data:
.IR $MUX_SHARE/themes/<name> .theme,
each naming up to six styles (bar, current-window chip, active/inactive borders,
copy-mode selection, prompt; only bar/window/accent are required). It is
compiled into tmux
.B @theme-*
options by
.RB ' "mux themes" ',
run from the tmux fragment at server start and re-pushed on drift, so a theme
edit needs no manual step.
.I $MUX_SHARE/themes/defaults
names the global default
.RB ( "default " \fINAME\fR )
and how an unset theme is derived
.RB ( "derive hash" | off ).
With
.BR hash ,
a session with no
.B theme
of its own takes one deterministically from its NAME, so a project wears a
stable colour with nothing configured. Priority is explicit, then the context's,
then derived, then the global default. A user override in
.I $MUX_DIR/themes
wins.
.SH AGENTS
An agent,
.IR $MUX_SHARE/agents/<name> .agent,
is two lines: a
.B go
command and a
.B resume
command, each a shell command mux runs as the agent pane's process. Claude is
the default; add a CLI by dropping in a profile. One in
.I $MUX_DIR/agents
overrides a shipped one of the same name.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Provided by the tmux fragment (prefix table unless noted):
.TP
.BR ( ", " )
Cycle to the previous / next visible session (skipping hidden ones).
.TP
.B b
Jump to the longest-blocked session (\c
.BR next-blocked );
repeat to walk the urgency order.
.TP
.B B
Toggle back to the last session.
.TP
.BR r ", " R
Put the window's geometry back:
.B r
evens out the top row's pane WIDTHS and re-pins the bottom pane's HEIGHT;
.B R
additionally rebuilds the bottom pane, clearing a stuck RENDER state a repaint
cannot.
.IP
Both matter because tmux redistributes size proportionally on every resize, so
a window resized a few times drifts off the even split its layout asked for and
stays there \(em and border\-drag is unbound (a copy drag across a divider
would otherwise yank it), so nothing else puts it back.
.IP
Panes of differing HEIGHT are left alone: those are stacked rather than a single
row, and it declines rather than rearranging a layout it does not model.
.TP
.B Space
The explorer (\c
.BR choose-tree ).
.TP
.B click " (status)"
Click a session chip in the strip to jump straight to it, or the view
indicator at the right end to cycle how view tension is resolved.
.SH CONTEXTS AND PARTITIONS
mux core knows nothing about any particular notion of "context" (a
work/personal split, a namespace, a git host). It asks an OPTIONAL command for
one word and decides everything else itself.
.PP
.RS
.B context\-command
.I <cmd>
.RB "in " $MUX_DIR/config
.RE
.PP
A bare name (no slash) is looked up in
.B $MUX_DIR
before
.BR $PATH ,
so a config file shared between machines need not carry an absolute path. The
command prints a TOKEN; empty output or a non\-zero exit means
.BR global .
That is the entire integration surface: no sockets, no styles, no themes, no
path classification. The integrator reports IDENTITY; mux decides presentation
and isolation.
.PP
A
.B context
is a settings axis, named by the token. A
.B partition
is an isolation axis: sessions in different partitions are mutually invisible.
A context's partition defaults to its own token, so contexts are isolated by
default, but several may name one partition to share it. Everything
isolation\-scoped keys on the partition: the tmux socket, the agent\-state
directory, the discovery map, the session set, and profile visibility.
.PP
Settings resolve in three levels, merged last\-wins, unconditionally:
.TP
1.
mux's built\-in defaults \(em BEHAVIOUR only, never a location.
.TP
2.
.IR partitions/<name> .partition
\(em what this partition shares.
.TP
3.
.IR contexts/<token> .context
\(em what is unique to this context.
.PP
The same key set is legal in either file
.RB ( label ", " theme ", " derive ", " agent ", " layout ", " scan ,
.BR ignore ", " host\-chip ,
plus
.B partition
in a context), so WHERE you put a key is the statement of its scope and there
is no per\-key rule to learn.
.PP
The built\-in defaults carry no location keys. That is what stops
.B scan
leaking between partitions: a partition nobody configured gets no roots and so
no map, making a missing context file visible rather than quietly indexing the
wrong tree.
.I $MUX_SHARE/partitions/global.partition
ships with
.RB ' "scan ~/src 3" '.
.PP
.PP
.B ignore \fIPATTERN\fR
prunes DISCOVERY, which is how two repositories sharing a basename stop being
ambiguous without renaming either of them. A pattern CONTAINING a slash is a
glob against the whole absolute path
.RB ( "*/athena\-repos/*" );
one with NO slash matches a single path COMPONENT, so
.B ignore node_modules
means the obvious thing rather than silently matching nothing. It takes
.BR scan 's
multiplicity rule exactly: repeatable within a file, and a file that sets it
REPLACES the inherited list rather than extending it, so a context can drop
what its partition ignores.
.PP
It governs only what mux VOLUNTEERS. An explicit
.B mux go \fI~/some/ignored/repo\fR
still resolves, because a path you typed is evidence. A pattern matching
nothing is reported by
.BR "mux scan" :
a filter that silently does nothing is indistinguishable from one that works.
.PP
A token becomes a socket name and a path component and is validated as a DNS
label. An invalid one is an error, never a silent fall back: a typo'd token
quietly becoming the baseline would put work sessions in the personal
partition.
.PP
.B mux only reflects a context; it enforces nothing.
Whatever backs a boundary (a Unix group, an ACL, a namespace) lives in whatever
supplies the token. The banner is a reminder, never permission.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
A desktop notification fires when an agent stops needing the CPU and starts
needing YOU: a real transition out of
.BR working ,
into
.B blocked
(a permission prompt) or
.B idle
(the turn finished). Not on a same-state repeat, not on the way INTO
.B working
\(em that transition is you \(em and not while you are already looking at the
pane, unless the profile says
.BR "notify always" .
.PP
The notification is raised at
.B normal
urgency, never
.BR critical .
critical means "never expire" to mako and dunst alike, so it produced a banner
that outlived the prompt it announced and had to be dismissed by hand. The
status strip and the tray already hold the state persistently; the notification
only has to be the nudge that points at them.
.PP
mux clears its own banner once that state passes, and when a pane dies still
holding one. Clearing calls the freedesktop
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications.CloseNotification
method rather than any one daemon's CLI, trying
.BR gdbus ,
.BR busctl ,
.BR dbus\-send ,
then
.B makoctl
last. So it cleans up under X11 and Wayland alike, wherever a spec-compliant
daemon runs. Raising needs
.BR notify\-send .
Both halves are optional and best effort: a box with neither is one where mux
notifies nobody, which is never an error.
.SH TMUX INTEGRATION
Source the shipped fragments from your
.IR tmux.conf :
.PP
.nf
.RS
source-file ~/.local/share/mux/mux.tmux
source-file ~/.local/share/mux/mux-opinions.tmux   # optional
.RE
.fi
.PP
.I mux.tmux
is required \(em the status bar, feature bindings, and resize hooks; it reaches
mux's helpers only as
.RB ' "mux <verb>" ',
so it carries no paths.
.I mux-opinions.tmux
is optional ergonomics (mouse, scroll routing, navigation).
.SH CAPABILITIES
.B mux capabilities
prints what this mux can do, so a consumer never has to sniff a version. The
first line is
.BR "mux <version>" ,
then one
.B "NAME VALUE [unavailable]"
per capability, parsed with a single
.BR "read -r name value note" .
It always exits 0: it is a pure report, and a non\-zero exit would be
indistinguishable from the transport.
.PP
.EX
mux 0.32
capabilities 1
agent\-list 1
agent\-summary 1
agent\-doctor 1
check 1
notify 1 unavailable
context 1
latch 1
attach\-only 1
latch\-fallback no
.EE
.PP
THE WHOLE KNOWN SET IS ENUMERATED, including what this build does not implement.
A present\-only list makes absence ambiguous: a consumer cannot tell "this mux
does not support it" from "whoever added the verb forgot the line". Declaring
everything makes absence mean exactly one thing \(em that this mux predates the
capability itself \(em and gives three answers instead of two:
.TP
.I <n>
Usable here. The integer is the INTERFACE version, not the release: it is bumped
when the output or the semantics change, never for an implementation change.
Presence alone would not be enough, since
.B agent-list
could keep its name and go back to emitting an epoch instead of an age, which is
exactly the property a cross\-machine reader depends on.
.TP
.B unavailable
Implemented, but not usable in this context \(em
.B notify
with no send backend,
.B context
with no
.B context-command
configured. A later call may answer differently.
.TP
.B no
This build does not have it at all. It is a FORWARD declaration, not padding:
.B latch\-fallback
is listed because
.B mux latch
deliberately drives one transport, with an ordered fallback list left as a later
refactor, so a caller wanting several can ASK instead of discovering by failure.
.IP
.B attach\-only
was this entry one release ago and now reads
.BR 1 ,
which is the lifecycle working as intended: a declared absence became a declared
presence, and no consumer had to sniff a version to notice.
.PP
For a mux too old to know the verb, the answer is exit 2 (see EXIT STATUS): a
caller should then assume only what it can see. That is the case this verb
exists for, measured on a fleet part way through an upgrade, where a reachable
and perfectly healthy box answered
.B "unknown verb: agent-list"
and read as broken.
.PP
Internal verbs are deliberately absent from the report, but they are still
CLASSIFIED in the manifest, and
.I test/mux-capabilities.t
asserts every dispatchable verb appears there. So a new verb nobody classified
fails the suite rather than quietly never being advertised \(em omission is the
one failure a curated list cannot catch.
.SH LATCH
.B mux latch " " \fIHOST\fR [: \fISESSION\fR ]
keeps an attachment to a remote mux session alive as the network allows.
.PP
BRING YOUR OWN TRANSPORT. mux owns the state machine and the attach semantics;
ssh, mosh, Eternal Terminal or anything else supplies the pipe. latch never
carries a keystroke and knows nothing about hosts, addresses or MTUs \(em it
decides WHEN to re\-establish an attachment and says WHY it has not, which is
what keeps it from growing into a transport project.
.PP
THE FIRST FIELD IS ALWAYS THE HOST, and the colon is optional. Everything after
the FIRST colon is the session name, so a name may itself contain one. What
latch asks the far side to run follows from that:
.PP
.EX
mux latch box            \(->  mux resume
mux latch box:api        \(->  mux go api
mux latch box:           \(->  mux resume
.EE
.PP
WITH NO SESSION NAMED the command is
.BR "mux resume" ,
which rebuilds what that box had and attaches the first. That is the right verb
BECAUSE of how it creates: after a reboot it restores the sessions you actually
had, where
.B go
would build a single empty one. It is deterministic, so a retry lands in the
same place, and idempotent, so retrying costs nothing.
.PP
Attach\-only therefore applies to the NAMED form only, and nothing is lost:
.B resume
already never invents anything, since it only rebuilds what was recorded.
.SS The state machine
The question that partitions it is not which exit code came back, but: does
resolving this need a HUMAN, or PATIENCE? Retrying is right in every state
except one, where it is actively harmful.
.TP
.B probing
Down, recoverable with no human action. Retried on a backoff.
.TP
.B blocked
No credential is live YET \(em an empty agent, a key not added, a hardware key
waiting for a touch. latch DOES NOT ATTEMPT, because every attempt is a prompt
and a loop here is a prompt storm. It polls the CREDENTIAL instead, which costs
nothing (\c
.B ssh\-add \-l
answers in about 12ms), raises no prompt while it waits, and moves on by itself
the moment a key appears. So it is a waiting state, not a dead end.
.TP
.B denied
A credential WAS offered and the far side rejected it. The opposite handling:
TERMINAL. latch cannot observe a human fixing
.IR authorized_keys ,
so there is no condition on which to retry, and retrying a rejected credential
forever is the same storm wearing a backoff. A HOST KEY refusal lands here too:
it is the same refusal pointing the other way \(em a rebuilt host presents a new
key and mux rejects it \(em and no amount of patience resolves it either.
.TP
.B unknown
Cannot be determined. Never reported as usable and never as blocked; retried
with more patience. On a real fleet this is common rather than exotic: an MTU
blackhole passes every cheap liveness probe there is.
.TP
.B ended
The human detached or quit. TERMINAL, and latch exits 0 \(em retrying would
resurrect the session they just closed.
.TP
.B gone
The far side no longer has that session. Reported loudly, never silently
recreated.
.TP
.B refused
The far side answered, and no amount of patience helps. Its mux does not know
the verb (exit 2, a version answer rather than a transport failure), or the
remote SHELL could not run mux at all (127 not found, 126 not executable).
Those codes are attributable only because mux itself uses none of them: see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
127 is the common one on a fresh box, and latch retried it forever until a live
run against a second machine showed it.
.PP
Only one latch runs per target at a time. A network flap can fire several
attempts at once \(em a hook, a key binding, a human \(em and N loops against
one target is N credential prompts and N reconnect races. A stale lock from a
killed run is reclaimed by checking the recorded pid.
.SS Seams
Five, each a command, each overridable by environment or by a
.IR MUX_DIR /config
key, because every one of them encodes transport\-specific knowledge that does
not belong in mux.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_TRANSPORT " (" latch\-transport )
Stand the pipe up. The template is split into ARGV on whitespace and each word
substituted separately; nothing is eval'd. Default
.BR "ssh \-t %h sh \-lc %q" .
.RS
.TP
.B %h
the host
.TP
.B %s
the session, or the host when none was named. Prefer
.B %c
or
.BR %q ,
which carry the whole command and get the no\-session case right.
.TP
.B %c
the remote mux command, composed by latch, as exactly ONE argv element
.TP
.B %q
the same command, additionally shell\-quoted
.TP
.B %%
a literal percent
.RE
.IP
.B %c
AND
.B %q
BOTH EXIST because transports differ on whether argv survives, and that was
measured rather than assumed.
.B "ssh host sh \-lc 'mux \-V'"
does not run
.BR "mux \-V" :
ssh CONCATENATES its remaining arguments into one string and the remote login
shell re\-splits it, so the inner shell receives
.B mux
with
.B \-V
as its
.B $0
and runs the bare session picker. Passing the command shell\-quoted instead
prints the version, correctly. So
.B %c
suits a transport that preserves argv
.RB ( "docker exec" ,
.BR "kubectl exec" )
and
.B %q
one that flattens to a string and re\-parses remotely (ssh).
.IP
.B "sh \-lc"
in the default is load\-bearing, not decoration: sshd runs a remote command
WITHOUT a login shell, so
.B "ssh host mux go x"
gets a PATH with no
.I ~/.local/bin
and exits 127
.BR "mux: not found" .
A login shell reads the profile that puts mux on PATH.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_AUTH " (" latch\-auth )
Is a credential live? Answers 0 yes, 1 needs a human, 78 cannot tell. Defaults
to the shipped
.BR ssh\-auth ,
which consults a live control master and then
.BR ssh\-add ;
neither connects, and together they cost about 12ms.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_PROBE " (" latch\-probe )
Is the target usable? Same three answers. NO PROBE MEANS NO OPINION, and that is
the default: latch just attempts, and the attempt is the probe. That is a
different instruction from a probe that ran and could not tell, which waits, and
conflating the two once made a stock latch wait forever and never attach. mux
SHIPS
.I share/latch/ssh\-probe
without wiring it; shipping a hook and running it are different acts.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_CLASSIFY " (" latch\-classify )
Given
.I "<exit> <stderr\-file>"
print one state word. Defaults to the shipped
.BR ssh\-classify ,
which is ssh\-specific by necessity: ssh exits 255 for a refused credential, an
unreachable host and a killed connection alike, so stderr is the only
discriminator there is. That specificity is precisely why this is a seam and not
core code.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_STATUS " (" latch\-status )
Called with
.IR "<state> <detail>" ,
fire and forget. Milestones also go to the terminal unconditionally, because
"why is my terminal dead" is the actual daily complaint and silence is the thing
being fixed.
.PP
78 means CANNOT ANSWER, and any other unrecognised code is read the same way.
A hook that failed in a way it did not anticipate has not answered, and guessing
on its behalf is how an edge nobody checked gets reported as fine. A seam left
UNSET is a different thing again: it means no opinion, so the caller proceeds.
.PP
A hook NAMED but not found is a configuration error and latch exits 2 saying so,
rather than carrying on. Silently continuing without a classifier puts every
attempt in
.B unknown
and retries forever against a host that is perfectly fine.
.SS The hook library
A hook is NAMED, not spelled out, and a bare name resolves
.IR $MUX_DIR /latch
first, then
.IR $MUX_SHARE /latch,
then PATH \(em the same overlay\-over\-shipped order layouts, themes and agents
use, and for the reason
.B context\-command
already documents: this config travels between machines through a dotfiles repo,
so it must not carry an absolute path that is right on only one of them. Only
the first WORD is resolved, since a hook value is a command line; a value
containing a slash is a path already and is left alone.
.PP
Three ship, in
.IR share/latch :
.BR ssh\-auth ,
.B ssh\-classify
and
.BR ssh\-probe .
Only ssh ones, deliberately: a mosh or Eternal Terminal hook is not shipped
because it is not verified here, the same reason
.I share/agents/gemini.agent
declares no busy marker. Adding one is a FILE, not a patch, which is the whole
point of the arrangement.
.PP
Each is a small program with a header explaining what it can and cannot answer.
.B ssh\-probe
is worth reading before wiring it: a positive answer rules out one failure and
leaves the interesting one untouched, because an MTU blackhole passes every
cheap liveness signal there is.
.PP
ONE TRANSPORT AT A TIME, deliberately. State is tracked per transport, so an
ordered fallback list is a later refactor rather than a rewrite:
.B blocked
is a per\-transport verdict, and across several transports the aggregate is
blocked only when every one of them is.
.SS Attach\-only on retry
The first attempt may CREATE, since you asked to latch onto something. Every
attempt after it asks for
.BR "mux go \-\-attach\-only" ,
which refuses rather than creates, and the classifier turns the far side's own
refusal into
.BR gone .
A reattach that finds nothing means the host rebooted, and handing over a fresh
empty session where the work was is the worst available answer: it looks like
success and the work is gone.
.PP
NEGOTIATED ONCE, LAZILY, and this is the first real consumer of
.BR "mux capabilities" .
A remote too old for the flag answers exit 2 with a usage block, so using it
blind would make latch work perfectly until the first drop and then break \(em
exactly the discover\-by\-failure the handshake exists to end. The query is
deferred to the first RETRY, so the happy path pays nothing, and cached for the
run. A remote that cannot do it degrades to the older behaviour rather than to
nothing, because a stale far side is a reason to create, not to stop.
.PP
A transport template written with
.B %s
instead of
.B %c
or
.B %q
keeps the always\-creating behaviour, so this is opt\-in by template.
.PP
A failure to REACH the far side is never cached as a capability verdict. The
transport failing says nothing whatever about the remote's version, so the
question stays open and is asked again \(em the same rule as everywhere else
here: cannot tell is not no.
.SH EXIT STATUS
mux uses exactly three codes, and the absence of every other one is part of the
contract.
.TP
.B 0
It answered, or succeeded. An EMPTY answer is still an answer:
.B mux agent-list
on a host with no agents prints nothing and exits 0, which is a different fact
from a failure and must not be conflated with one.
.TP
.B 1
Refused, for a stated reason. Something is on stderr saying why, prefixed
.BR mux: .
A guard fired, drift was found, or the named thing does not exist.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, or a verb this mux does not know.
.PP
THE ABSENCE OF EVERYTHING ELSE IS LOAD-BEARING.
.B ssh
returns 255 for its own errors and otherwise passes the remote command's status
through, and a shell returns 126 and 127 for not-executable and not-found.
Because mux never produces any of those, a caller can attribute them with
certainty:
.PP
.EX
ssh host mux agent-list
  0      an answer (possibly empty)
  1      mux refused; the reason is on stderr
  2      that mux is too old to know the verb
  255    the transport, not mux
  127    mux is not on that host's PATH
.EE
.PP
That is how a fleet part way through an upgrade stops reading as half\-broken:
.B 2
is a VERSION answer, not a failure. It is also what a reconnect supervisor
classifies retries on. None of it survives one verb returning 3, so do not add
a code: widen an existing meaning, or put the detail on stderr.
.PP
Held by two tests.
.I test/mux-exit.t
pins what today's verbs return, including that every refusal also SAYS why;
.I test/lint.t
rejects any literal exit outside 0, 1 and 2 anywhere in shipped code, which is
the half that holds against code nobody has written yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MUX_DIR
User config / overrides (layouts, theme overrides, and
.IR config ,
which names the
.B context\-command
). Default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mux ,
else
.IR ~/.config/mux .
.TP
.B MUX_SHARE
Shipped package data (themes, layouts, agents, the tmux fragments). Default the
.I share
sibling of the
.B mux
binary's prefix.
.TP
.B MUX_CACHE
Per-socket palette stamp directory. Default
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/mux .
.TP
.BR MUX_NOTIFY_SEND ", " MUX_NOTIFY_CLOSE
Replace the built-in notification path, for a platform that is not freedesktop.
.B MUX_NOTIFY_SEND
is invoked as
.RI "CMD " "URGENCY SUMMARY BODY"
and should print an id on stdout;
.B MUX_NOTIFY_CLOSE
is invoked as
.RI "CMD " ID
with whatever send printed. The id is opaque to mux, so any token the pair
agrees on works. Set both or neither;
.B mux check
reports which path is live.
.TP
.B MUX_STYLE_PROC
Relocate
.I /proc
for
.BR mux-style ,
which reads the pane's foreground process group from
.IR /proc/<pid>/stat .
A TEST SEAM. Without it that arithmetic is unreachable under test: a test shell
has no controlling terminal, so tpgid is \-1, the non\-numeric guard fires and
the field is never read \(em so a wrong field index passes unnoticed, which it
did. Same seam, and the same reason, as
.BR MUX_DOCTOR_PROC .
.TP
.B MUX_NOTIF_TTL
Seconds after which a recorded notification id is considered stale and dropped
rather than closed. Default 3600.
.IP
A freedesktop id is a small integer the daemon assigns sequentially and REUSES
after it restarts, so an id held long enough stops naming our banner and starts
naming somebody else's. mux carries an id per STATE, not per notification, so a
session that finishes and is never touched again keeps its id indefinitely
\(em found on one holding an id for 32 hours, which the next transition would
have dutifully closed.
.IP
Dropping a stale id costs nothing, and that is only true because mux sends
.B normal
urgency, which expires on its own. Were that ever changed back to
.BR critical ,
which never expires, this would become a leak instead of a safeguard.
.TP
.BR MUX_LATCH_TRANSPORT ", " MUX_LATCH_AUTH ", " MUX_LATCH_PROBE ", " \
MUX_LATCH_CLASSIFY ", " MUX_LATCH_STATUS
The five
.B mux latch
seams, each a command and each also settable as a
.IR latch\- *
key in
.IR $MUX_DIR/config ,
where the environment wins. Described under
.BR LATCH .
.BR MUX_LATCH_BACKOFF ,
.BR MUX_LATCH_BACKOFF_MAX ,
.B MUX_LATCH_BLOCKED_WAIT
and
.B MUX_LATCH_MAX_TRIES
bound the retry loop (0 tries means forever), and
.B MUX_LATCH_SLEEP
replaces
.BR sleep (1),
which is what lets the state machine be tested without waiting.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/<name>.profile
User session profiles (\c
.BR save " writes here)."
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/config
Invariants; today just
.BR context\-command .
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/partitions/<name>.partition
Settings shared by everything in a partition.
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/contexts/<token>.context
Settings unique to one context.
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/profiles
The profile TABLE: one line per session that deviates from what mux derives.
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/profiles.d/<name>.profile
A profile that outgrew one line (comments, or a value containing a space).
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/scan
Discovery roots, one
.RI ' "PATH [DEPTH]" '
per line. Absent,
.I ~/src
is used when it exists.
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/hosts
Per-host colours for the status-left host chip, so identically-named sessions
on different machines are told apart. One
.RI ' "HOST STYLE" '
per line, where HOST is
.RB ' "hostname \-s" '
and STYLE is a COMMA-joined tmux run
.RB ( fg=colour230,bg=#5f3a1a ).
The file is optional and holds only the hosts you want to pin: an unlisted host
gets a stable, readable colour derived from its name.
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/{themes,layouts,agents}
Overrides for shipped themes, layouts and agents, and any of your own.
.TP
.I $MUX_SHARE/{themes,layouts,agents}
Shipped palette, layouts, and agents.
.TP
.I $MUX_SHARE/layouts/default.layout
The arrangement
.B go
builds when a profile names no other.
.TP
.I $MUX_SHARE/mux.tmux
The tmux integration fragment.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open (or attach) a session for the current project, agent continuing:
.PP
.RS
.B mux go
.RE
.PP
Name a session for a directory, then bring it up resuming a conversation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cd ~/src/api && mux new api
mux go \-\-resume api
.RE
.fi
.PP
Jump to whoever has been blocked longest, then back:
.PP
.RS
prefix
.BR b " \(em ... \(em prefix " B
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
